   /**
    * Inventories held through a corruption death so they can be handed back.
    *
    * <p>Dying to the Mindbinder used to cost a player everything they carried -
    * including the totem it had already torn out of their pack, which is the one
    * death in the mod where the killer is a script that never has to beat you.
    * A held inventory is written straight back in {@link #restoreCorruptionDeath}
    * once the death has landed, so the death is real and the walk back is real,
    * but nothing is lost. Entries expire (a totem can leave a player alive with a
    * hold pending) and are pruned on the mind tick.
    */
   private static final Map<UUID, CorruptionHold> corruptionDeathHolds = new HashMap<>();
   private static final long CORRUPTION_HOLD_MILLIS = 8_000L;
   /**
    * A held pack on its way across a respawn, and when to give up on it.
    *
    * <p>A death is not a teleport: vanilla builds a <b>new</b> {@code ServerPlayer} for the
    * respawn and only copies the inventory across when the keep-inventory rule says to or when
    * the body that died was a spectator. A corruption death owns neither - the slots were never
    * emptied and the rule was never asked - so a pack written back into the dying body stayed in
    * the corpse and the player came back with nothing. That is the whole of "the corruption 3
    * death still takes your items", and it is why the copy is parked here for the body that
    * follows rather than written into the one that is about to be thrown away.
    *
    * <p>Long-lived on purpose - a player reads the death screen - and spent exactly once, filling
    * only the slots that are still empty, so a death that did carry the pack across (a
    * spectator's, or a keep-inventory world) is never paid for twice.
    */
   private static final Map<UUID, CorruptionHold> pendingRespawnPacks = new HashMap<>();
   private static final long RESPAWN_PACK_MILLIS = 600_000L;
